  ],
  "difficulty": 4,
  "marks": 3,
  "estimatedSeconds": 270,
  "stem": "Show that the equation $x^2 + (k - 1)x - k = 0$ has real roots for all real values of $k$.",
  "answerSpec": {
   "markScheme": {
    "points": [
     {
      "id": "p1",
      "description": "M1 forms the discriminant $(k - 1)^2 - 4(1)(-k)$.",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p2",
      "description": "M1 simplifies to $k^2 + 2k + 1 = (k + 1)^2$.",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p3",
      "description": "A1 states $(k + 1)^2 \\geq 0$ for all real $k$, so the roots are always real.",
      "marks": 1
     }
    ]
   },
   "modelAnswer": "$$\nb^2 - 4ac = (k - 1)^2 - 4(1)(-k)\n$$\n\n$$\n= k^2 - 2k + 1 + 4k = k^2 + 2k + 1 = (k + 1)^2\n$$\n\nA square is never negative, so $b^2 - 4ac \\geq 0$ for all real $k$ and the roots are always real (equal when $k = -1$)."
  },
  "markingMode": "MANUAL",
  "explanation": "The discriminant is a perfect square, so it is never negative. (In fact the equation factorises as $(x + k)(x - 1) = 0$.)",
  "hints": [
   "Take $c = -k$, so $-4ac = +4k$."
  ],
  "skill": "ANALYSIS",
